Agreed. Recognition means more when it tracks something real.

If someone has earned competence, trust, rank, mastery, or victory through repeated performance under meaningful constraints, flattening that into “everyone is the same” can erase the very work that made the distinction legitimate.

The cleaner rule is:

\text{respect dignity universally}

while still allowing

\text{status, authority, trust, or reward} \propto \text{demonstrated merit in the relevant domain}

That also means the standard should be visible enough to inspect. Otherwise “earned” can quietly become favoritism, mythology, or inherited reputation.

So yes: honor what was actually earned, preserve the lineage of how it was earned, and don’t counterfeit the signal.

“Pays to be a winner” only has meaning if winner still costs something.